steps.body:
A utility type is a generic type that comes with TypeScript. You pass it a type, and it gives back a new one. Three of them change every property at once:

- `Partial<T>` makes every property optional. That's the shape of a patch: any subset of the fields.
- `Required<T>` makes every property required. That's the shape of settings once the defaults are filled in.
- `Readonly<T>` marks every property `readonly`, so assigning to one is a type error.

```typescript types
type User = { id: number; name: string; email: string };

type UserPatch = Partial<User>;
//   ^? type UserPatch = { id?: number; name?: string; email?: string; }
type FrozenUser = Readonly<User>;
//   ^? type FrozenUser = { readonly id: number; readonly name: string; readonly email: string; }
```

All three change the top level only, so a nested object keeps its own types. And `Readonly` exists only for the checker. Node strips it like any other type, so call `Object.freeze` as well when the value must not change while the program runs.

examples.code:
type User = { id: number; name: string; email: string };

function updateUser(user: User, patch: Partial<User>): User {
  return { ...user, ...patch };
}

function show(user: User): string {
  return `#${user.id} ${user.name} <${user.email}>`;
}

const ada: User = { id: 1, name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com" };
const renamed = updateUser(ada, { name: "Ada Lovelace" });
const moved = updateUser(renamed, { email: "ada@lovelace.dev" });

console.log(show(ada));
console.log(show(renamed));
console.log(show(moved));

examples.explanation:
`Partial<User>` accepts any subset of a user's fields, so one function handles a new name and a new email alike. The spread copies `user`, then the fields in `patch` overwrite the ones they name. Spreading builds a new object, which is why `ada` still prints as it was.

A patch is still checked against `User`. A field that `User` doesn't have is an error, and so is `undefined` for a field, because this course turns on `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`. So a patch can never blank out a name:

```typescript harness
type User = { id: number; name: string; email: string };

function updateUser(user: User, patch: Partial<User>): User {
  return { ...user, ...patch };
}

const ada: User = { id: 1, name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com" };
{{code}}
```

```typescript error
updateUser(ada, { name: undefined });
```

